        // Call initialize as external caller; initializer should accept this call and return (not revert).
        bool ok;
        try c.initialize(accessControl) {
            ok = true;
        } catch {
            ok = false;
        }

        // The correct contract's proxiableUUID() has modifier notDelegated which reverts when called
        // directly (address(this) == __self), so calling it on the implementation must revert.
        // We assert the reference always makes proxiableUUID revert when invoked on implementation.
        // The mutant may alter initialization state leading to different proxy checks later; the fuzzer
        // can find an input where proxiableUUID does NOT revert (buggy behavior). We require initialize to have returned successfully
        // (ok == true) for the scenario to be admissible.
        if (!ok) {
            // If initialize itself reverted, this input is not admissible for our rule — skip by assuming false.
            // We use vm.assume(false) by reverting the test early via an assertion that always passes for skipping.
            // Instead we simply return (the framework treats this as a non-failing test case); but tests must assert exactly one thing below,
            // so returning early is acceptable.
            return;
